    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(11))  # F0..F10\n    customers = list(range(5))  # C0..C4\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(39, 54), (44, 32), (98, 78), (15, 0), (73, 78), (60, 90), (79, 92), (48, 88), (48, 30), (53, 89), (72, 31)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(9, 66), (26, 3), (68, 89), (18, 47), (78, 59)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [155.0, 122.0, 185.0, 196.0, 53.0, 134.0, 125.0, 113.0, 52.0, 98.0, 116.0]\n    customer_demands = [42.0, 33.0, 49.0, 21.0, 28.0]\n    fixed_costs = [245.0, 379.0, 491.0, 427.0, 418.0, 258.0, 204.0, 147.0, 394.0, 268.0, 449.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Deciding Which Warehouses to Open and How to Serve Customers at the Lowest Total Cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at a point where we need to make a practical decision about where to place our warehouses and how to move product to customers in a way that keeps service dependable and total cost low. We have a set of potential warehouse sites and a set of customer destinations plotted on a map. The candidate warehouse sites are mapped in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (39, 54)\n  - F1: (44, 32)\n  - F2: (98, 78)\n  - F3: (15, 0)\n  - F4: (73, 78)\n  - F5: (60, 90)\n  - F6: (79, 92)\n  - F7: (48, 88)\n  - F8: (48, 30)\n  - F9: (53, 89)\n  - F10: (72, 31), and the customer destinations are mapped in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (9, 66)\n  - C1: (26, 3)\n  - C2: (68, 89)\n  - C3: (18, 47)\n  - C4: (78, 59). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates so we\u2019re consistent about how distance translates into shipping cost.\n\nHere\u2019s the challenge in plain terms:\n- Opening a warehouse isn\u2019t free. Each site comes with a one-time opening expense, detailed in [245.0, 379.0, 491.0, 427.0, 418.0, 258.0, 204.0, 147.0, 394.0, 268.0, 449.0]. We can only ship from a site if we decide to open it.\n- Shipping costs grow with how far we send product and how much we send. The current transport rate is 0.5 per kilometer per unit shipped, applied to the Euclidean distances between the chosen warehouses and the customers they serve.\n- Every warehouse has a limit to what it can handle. Those capacity limits are specified in [155.0, 122.0, 185.0, 196.0, 53.0, 134.0, 125.0, 113.0, 52.0, 98.0, 116.0], and we cannot exceed them.\n- Every customer must receive their full requirement. Those demand levels are summarized in [42.0, 33.0, 49.0, 21.0, 28.0], and we need to plan shipments so that each customer is fully served.\n\nOur goal is straightforward: minimize total cost, combining the fixed costs of opening selected warehouses with the variable shipping costs, while honoring capacity limits and meeting all customer demand. In practice, this means evaluating the trade-off between opening more sites to shorten shipping distances versus opening fewer sites to avoid fixed costs, and then allocating shipments from the open locations to customers in a way that uses capacity efficiently.\n\nWhat you can expect from this exercise is a recommended set of warehouse openings and a shipping plan that spells out which customers are served from which locations and in what quantities, along with a clear cost breakdown. We\u2019ll also highlight the key sensitivities\u2014such as how changes in demand patterns, capacity, or transport rate could shift the optimal setup\u2014so the decision remains robust as conditions evolve.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Deciding Which Warehouses to Open and How to Serve Customers at the Lowest Total Cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at a point where we need to make a practical decision about where to place our warehouses and how to move product to customers in a way that keeps service dependable and total cost low. We have a set of potential warehouse sites and a set of customer destinations plotted on a map. The candidate warehouse sites are mapped in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer destinations are mapped in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates so we\u2019re consistent about how distance translates into shipping cost.\n\nHere\u2019s the challenge in plain terms:\n- Opening a warehouse isn\u2019t free. Each site comes with a one-time opening expense, detailed in {FIXED_COSTS}. We can only ship from a site if we decide to open it.\n- Shipping costs grow with how far we send product and how much we send. The current transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE} per kilometer per unit shipped, applied to the Euclidean distances between the chosen warehouses and the customers they serve.\n- Every warehouse has a limit to what it can handle. Those capacity limits are specified in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and we cannot exceed them.\n- Every customer must receive their full requirement. Those demand levels are summarized in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}, and we need to plan shipments so that each customer is fully served.\n\nOur goal is straightforward: minimize total cost, combining the fixed costs of opening selected warehouses with the variable shipping costs, while honoring capacity limits and meeting all customer demand. In practice, this means evaluating the trade-off between opening more sites to shorten shipping distances versus opening fewer sites to avoid fixed costs, and then allocating shipments from the open locations to customers in a way that uses capacity efficiently.\n\nWhat you can expect from this exercise is a recommended set of warehouse openings and a shipping plan that spells out which customers are served from which locations and in what quantities, along with a clear cost breakdown. We\u2019ll also highlight the key sensitivities\u2014such as how changes in demand patterns, capacity, or transport rate could shift the optimal setup\u2014so the decision remains robust as conditions evolve."
